{"name":"Camel Train","hex":"#baae9d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"camel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mammalian beast of burden, much used in desert areas, of the genus Camelus.","A light brownish color, like that of a camel (also called camel brown)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a light brown color like that of a camel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camel"},{"term":"train","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Elongated or trailing portion.\nThe elongated back portion of a dress or skirt (or an ornamental piece of material added to similar effect), which drags along the ground. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","The elongated back portion of a dress or skirt (or an ornamental piece of material added to similar effect), which drags along the ground. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To practice an ability.","To teach and form (someone) by practice; to educate (someone)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/train"}],"entries":[{"title":"Camel train","blocks":[{"text":"A camel train, caravan, or camel string is a series of camels carrying passengers and goods on a regular or semi-regular service between points. Despite rarely travelling faster than human walking speed, for centuries camels' ability to withstand harsh conditions made them ideal for communication and trade in the desert areas of North Africa and the Arabian Peninsula. Camel trains were also used sparingly elsewhere around the globe. Since the early 20th century they have been largely replaced by motorized vehicles or air traffic."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Salt_transport_by_a_camel_train_on_Lake_Assale_%28Karum%29_in_Ethiopia.jpg/330px-Salt_transport_by_a_camel_train_on_Lake_Assale_%28Karum%29_in_Ethiopia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel_train","wikidata":"Q4149527"},{"title":"Camel","blocks":[{"text":"A camel is an even-toed ungulate in the genus Camelus that bears distinctive fatty deposits known as \"humps\" on its back. Camels have long been domesticated and, as livestock, they provide food and textiles. Camels are working animals especially suited to their desert habitat and are a vital means of transport for passengers and cargo. There are three surviving species of camel. The one-humped dromedary makes up 94% of the world's camel population, and the two-humped Bactrian camel makes up 6%. The wild Bactrian camel is a distinct species that is not ancestral to the domestic Bactrian camel, and is now endangered, with around 1,000 individuals and a continuing decline."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg/330px-07._Camel_Profile%2C_near_Silverton%2C_NSW%2C_07.07.2007.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel","wikidata":"Q7375"},{"title":"Train","blocks":[{"text":"A train is a series of connected vehicles that run along a railway track and transport people or freight. Trains are typically pulled or pushed by locomotives, though some are self-propelled, such as multiple units or railcars. Passengers and cargo are carried in railroad cars, also known as wagons or carriages. Trains are designed to a certain gauge, or distance between rails. Most trains operate on steel tracks with steel wheels, the low friction of which makes them more efficient than other forms of transport. Many countries use rail transport."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B4_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D1%84%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%8B_%D0%A8%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5._%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B6%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D1%8C.jpg/330px-%D0%9F%D0%BE%D0%B5%D0%B7%D0%B4_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D1%84%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%8B_%D0%A8%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5._%D0%92%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B6%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BB%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D1%8C.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Train","wikidata":"Q870"},{"title":"Camel (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Camel is a color that resembles the color of the hair of a camel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Camelcalf-feeding.jpg/330px-Camelcalf-feeding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camel_(color)","wikidata":"Q17082553"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/BosraMosaicCamels.png/500px-BosraMosaicCamels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/BosraMosaicCamels.png/500px-BosraMosaicCamels.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ancient Roman mosaic depicting a merchant leading a camel train. Bosra, Syria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABosraMosaicCamels.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/ST-caravan.jpg/500px-ST-caravan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/ST-caravan.jpg/500px-ST-caravan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Caravan Approaching a City in the Vast Desert of Sahara\", from: Stanley and the White Heroes in Africa, by H. B. Scammel, 1890","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AST-caravan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/CamelTrainKalgoorlie_WEFretwellCollection.jpg/500px-CamelTrainKalgoorl